From 9fe605d0ac57f84452a9f9151f30ce7b8c2cfef8 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: elliotttate <elliotttate@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 1 Oct 2026 13:05:18 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] Aurora: up to 7 in-between frames, and none while the game
 runs slow

Frame interpolation could show 60 or 120 frames a second (1 or 3 in-between
frames per game frame). frame_interp::kMaxSteps is now 7, for a display's own
rate up to 240 Hz: the held frames, the per-job ranges (InterpRanges, which
held three and overran the helper's stack past that), the dump names and the
buffers' limits follow it. Each buffer still starts at what 60 and 120 used
and grows only to what a frame needs. The vertex limit is a 256-byte multiple
per step: 8 MB / 3 * 7 was not a multiple of 4, no mapped staging buffer
could be made, and every frame went out without in-between frames.

The pacing dropped in-between frames when the GPU or the render worker fell
behind, but not when the game itself did. On a CPU with few cores their work
(each draw captured on the GX worker and matched on the helper, the render
worker drawing them again) takes cores the game's thread needs. On 4 of an
i9-13900KF's E-cores at 60 FPS the game ran in slow motion, 24-28 game frames
a second, against 30 with interpolation off. Game frames ending more than
35.5 ms apart on average (8 frames; gaps of a quarter second or more, a load
or a pause, start it again) now count as an overload at any step count,
unless pacing is off (DOL_AURORA_FRAME_INTERP_PACING=0). After such a drop the
calm needed before trying again doubles up to 2 minutes, not 30 s. A frame
with none queues no matching work, so a drop gives that CPU back. Same setup:
29.6-30.0 game frames a second, with interpolation off for the heavy views.

Unchanged where it already ran well: the in-between frame dumps at 60 FPS
(game frames 880-910) are byte for byte the release's, 62 of 62. With 7 steps
(a 240 Hz display simulated) every frame has its 7, each nearer the next real
frame by an even step.
